WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.775
An ordinary model answers by writing.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.829
Each word goes back in.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.299
So each word costs another pass.

4
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.801
Inside, the last position is a column of
numbers.

5
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.853
The word it wrote was just the largest
one.

6
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.533
Now tell the same model to write nothing.

7
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.021
It runs once.

8
00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:19.297
Nothing comes out.

9
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.756
Instead, you hand it the answers.

10
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.036
The options are input now.

11
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.288
It reads the column at the rows your
options name.

12
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.417
Every other row is computed, and never
read.

13
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.368
Those three numbers are the entire output.

14
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.839
Now the fork.

15
00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:36.709
Put every option on one page.

16
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.156
One pass reads all three of them.

17
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.449
Swap two, and the answer moves.

18
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.977
Position was an input all along.

19
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.819
Or give every option a page of its own.

20
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.491
Three passes now, one number each.

21
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:51.747
Swap them.

22
00:00:51.747 --> 00:00:53.105
Nothing moves at all.

23
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.089
That is the trade, and nobody gets both.

24
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.047
So ask a question that spans two options.

25
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.947
The pages never meet.

26
00:01:00.947 --> 00:01:02.431
There is nothing to find.

27
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.347
One choice in, one distribution out.

28
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.768
One pass.

29
00:01:06.768 --> 00:01:07.536
No words.
